---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pressure-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="pressure" from="bar" to="psi" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a pressure</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type into either box. Bar to psi and psi to bar are the same control worked in either direction.</li>
      <li>Choose from the SI units, the practical metric units, psi, or the reference units used in weather, vacuum and medicine.</li>
      <li>The chart underneath runs a whole column of values, which is quicker than converting a tyre placard one figure at a time.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Tyre pressures, the most common reason to be here</h2>
    <p>
      Car door placards are printed in psi in the US and UK, and in bar or kPa almost everywhere
      else. The conversion is 1 bar = 14.5038 psi = 100 kPa. An error by a factor of ten (220 kPa
      confused with 22 psi) is a real risk, so here is the range you will meet:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>psi</th><th>bar</th><th>kPa</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>28</td><td>1.93</td><td>193</td><td>Small car, lightly loaded</td></tr>
          <tr><td>32</td><td>2.21</td><td>221</td><td>Most passenger cars</td></tr>
          <tr><td>35</td><td>2.41</td><td>241</td><td>Passenger car, fully loaded</td></tr>
          <tr><td>44</td><td>3.03</td><td>303</td><td>Small van</td></tr>
          <tr><td>80</td><td>5.52</td><td>552</td><td>Light truck rear axle</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100</td><td>6.89</td><td>689</td><td>Road bicycle tyre</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      One thing no converter can fix: placard pressures are cold pressures. Driving heats the air
      and raises the reading by roughly 3 psi, so check before you set off, not after.
    </p>

    <h2>Which factors are exact</h2>
    <p>
      The pascal is the SI unit (one newton per square metre), and all metric units are an exact
      multiple of it. A bar is exactly 100,000 Pa, a hectopascal exactly 100 Pa, and a millibar is
      exactly the same size as a hectopascal, which is why meteorologists switched labels without
      changing any numbers. The standard atmosphere is defined as exactly 101,325 Pa, and the torr
      as exactly one 760th of that.
    </p>
    <p>
      The psi follows from the exact pound and inch: 6,894.757293168361 Pa. The millimetre of
      mercury is defined from a specified mercury density and standard gravity, giving
      133.322387415 Pa, which differs from a torr by about one part in seven million. No
      blood-pressure cuff can measure that difference.
    </p>

    <h2>Gauge pressure versus absolute pressure</h2>
    <p>
      Most instruments read gauge pressure: the amount above the surrounding air. A tyre gauge
      showing 32 psi means 32 psi more than atmospheric, so the absolute pressure inside the tyre is
      about 46.7 psi. US engineering writes this as psig for gauge and psia for absolute.
    </p>
    <p>
      A unit converter cannot tell which one you have, because gauge and absolute use identical
      units: the difference is the reference point, not the scale. If you mix a weather
      reading (absolute) with an equipment reading (gauge), add or subtract one atmosphere:
      101.325 kPa, 1.01325 bar, or 14.696 psi.
    </p>

    <h2>Reference: pressures worth knowing</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Pressure</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sea-level standard atmosphere</td><td>1 atm = 101.325 kPa = 14.696 psi = 1,013.25 hPa</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical high-pressure weather system</td><td>1,030 hPa</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Deep low / severe storm</td><td>950 hPa</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Healthy blood pressure</td><td>120/80 mmHg = 16.0/10.7 kPa</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Espresso machine brew pressure</td><td>9 bar = 130.5 psi</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Domestic mains water</td><td>2–5 bar = 29–73 psi</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ten metres under water</td><td>1 extra atm, so about 2 atm absolute</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
